    80	2.1. Establishing  a need for the map
    81	At this stage, we provide  the LLM with our research  objectives
    82	and contextual  information  (e.g., paper abstract)  to elicit proposals
    83	for research  questions  and complementary  information  (e.g., additional
    84	objectives).  We edit the questions  as input for the next stage.
    85	2.2. Study identification
    86	2.2.1. Search
    87	Proposed  strategy: Search  aims to identify  relevant  documents  con-
    88	cerning  the research  objective.  Along with defining  research  objectives,
    89	the search  is a complex  and iterative  process.  Creating  a search  strategy
    90	from a search  intent involves  understanding  the searcher’s  underlying
    91	intentions.  The strategy  must be both transparent  and reproducible.
    92	Even though  semantic  searches2are becoming  more common,  the need
    93	for reproducibility  supports  using Boolean  searches.  Boolean  searches
    94	necessitate  formulating  a search  strategy  using Boolean  expressions  and
    95	operators.  The prerequisite  is the identification  and combination  of
    96	relevant  terms.  We advocate  for an autonomous  agent with Human-in-
    97	the-Loop  to meet the demand  for reproducibility,  account  for human
    98	involvement  in the process,  and leverage  the potential  of new techno-
    99	logical  developments.  We propose  an architecture  that places human
   100	intervention  at the center and considers  three agents:
   101	1.Keyword  Identification  Agent: The Keyword  Identification  Agent
   102	identifies  relevant  terms and provides  them for further  research.
   103	Language  models  can support  the search  for semantically  similar
   104	terms.  Semantic  similarities  can, for instance,  help find terms
   105	commonly  used in the past but have largely  been replaced  by
   106	other terms today.  This step also serves to solidify  the focus of
   107	2Underlined  words are defined  in the supplementary  material.the study. For example,  in 3D printing,  the searcher’s  intention
   108	may be to search  at the conceptual  term level, the level of
   109	subtypes  (e.g., Stereolithography),  or supertypes  (e.g., Rapid
   110	Prototyping).
   111	2.Semantic  Search  Agent: The Semantic  Search  Agent uses a
   112	Retrieval-Augmented  Generation  (RAG) architecture  to suggest
   113	relevant  documents  based on semantic  similarities.  In this con-
   114	text, it may be helpful  to consider  a graph database  as a storage
   115	component,  allowing  for the incorporation  of links between
   116	documents  (e.g., through  citations).  This step does not select
   117	documents  but adjusts  the search  strategy.  Relevant  search  terms
   118	are then extracted  again from the selected  documents.
   119	3.Search  Strategy  Agent: The Search  Strategy  Agent formulates  the
   120	search  strategy  used to conduct  the research.
   121	The Keyword  Identification  Agent and the Semantic  Search  Agent
   122	thus specifically  support  a citation  pearl growing  strategy .
   123	Relevant  literature: Wang et al. [5] created  prompts  of different  com-
   124	plexities,  the most complex  including  a search  example  and requesting
   125	the use of PICO. Also, prompts  were refined  to request  modifications
   126	to increase  the relevance  of elements.  In addition,  so-called  guided
   127	prompts  were formulated  that separate  the search  construction  process
   128	into sub-steps.  The authors  used existing  SLRs as a basis to assess the
   129	generated  searches.  The results  show the potential  to use ChatGPT  for
   130	search  generation.  The F-Measure  shows better results  for author-based
   131	queries;  however,  GPT-generated  queries  result in less recall, i.e.more
   132	relevant  papers  are missed.  Additional  interesting  findings  were: (1)
   133	The use of PICO harms recall; (2) the use of existing  SLRs as a basis to
   134	assess the generated  searches;  (3) requesting  refinements  reduces  recall
   135	and improves  precision.
   136	2.2.2. Inclusion  and exclusion
   137	Promposed  strategy: Defining  inclusion  and exclusion  criteria  is cru-
   138	cial for capturing  the researcher’s  intention.  Traditional  prompt  engi-
   139	neering  techniques  may reach their limits here. We argue that searchInformation  and Software  Technology  178 (2025)  107611
   140	2
   141	
   142	--- Page 3 ---
   143	K. Petersen and J.M. Gerken
   144	intentions are often initially latent and manifest during the search
   145	process. Therefore, a continual learning system can be superior to hard-
   146	coded prompts. Users’ preferences can be derived from document inclu-
   147	sion and exclusion. Frameworks like DSPy can also be helpful by op-
   148	timizing prompts. Learning approaches require larger datasets, which
   149	researchers need to create iteratively.
   150	From a technical perspective, including and excluding documents is
   151	a classification problem. Researchers classify documents as relevant or
   152	not relevant. However, this approach lacks transparency, traceability,
   153	and human oversight. Therefore, language models have to explain the
   154	reasons for inclusion and exclusion. Chain-of-thoughts prompting is a
   155	possible approach to help researchers understand the decision-making
   156	process of the LLM. From our perspective, citations are indispensable.
   157	They allow the verification of arguments and increase traceability.
   158	Relevant literature: Huotala et al. [6] used GPT to simply abstracts
   159	of papers and compare the paper selection done by GPT with human
   160	raters. As found by the authors, a similar performance between humans
   161	and the LLMs is achieved when using One-shot , Few-shot , or Few-shot
   162	with Chain-of-Thought prompting. The authors also found improved
   163	performances when using GPT-4 compared to GPT-3.5 when using
   164	prompt optimization. Zero-shot prompting was not performing well
   165	compared to other prompt optimization approaches. The authors also
   166	highlight the benefit of having seed papers or prior SLRs as input for
   167	prompting. Guo et al. [7] repeated the selection process of existing
   168	SLRs, highlighting that GPT was good at excluding irrelevant papers
   169	but not as good at achieving a high recall. They highlighted the value
   170	of GPT as a support tool for reviewers to save time.
   171	2.3. Data extraction and classification
   172	Before discussing the data extraction and classification strategies,
   173	we would like to highlight that, given the degree of automation used,
   174	it is now possible to go beyond adaptive reading depth during manual
   175	screening and consider the complete papers as input for the analysis for
   176	deductive coding.
   177	1.Inductive coding: In inductive coding, the classification emerges
   178	as a consequence of the coding process. We suggest using
   179	topic modeling with the titles and abstracts as input to identify
   180	the topics and clusters of papers. A typical process is to create
   181	embeddings , reduce dimensions, cluster embeddings, and create
   182	topic representations. Such a process is, for example, supported
   183	by Bertopic , which supports a modular framework, allowing the
   184	exchange of the approaches used, for example, for clustering.
   185	2.Deductive coding: Given is a data extraction scheme (e.g., SWE-
   186	BOK categories). As was learned in the context of screening,
   187	examples are important. Hence, we suggest using One-shot or
   188	Few-shot prompting. As the complete PDF document is analyzed,
   189	a RAG architecture may be helpful, as it first identifies the most
   190	relevant parts of a document after document splitting before
   191	prompting the OpanAI API.
   192	Relevant literature: A solution for inductive coding was proposed
   193	by Wang et al. [8]. They used the aforementioned Bertopic library
   194	to review interdisciplinary research. They compute topic diversity and
   195	cohesion of topics to learn about interdisciplinary integration of publi-
   196	cations. Besides showing hierarchical clusters of topics, feature words ,
   197	and c-TF-IDF scores for terms within topics, they also included a
   198	temporal analysis. Petersen [9] used GPT-4 to judge whether studies are
   199	indeed case studies as claimed by study authors. The result showed that
   200	GPT-4 outperformed the authors (both primary and secondary studies).2.4. Visualization
   201	ChatGPT already provides options for data visualization by gener-
   202	ating source code and visualizing data. However, it is noticeable that
   203	further specialized applications are being developed. An example of this
   204	isLIDA from Microsoft.
   205	For exploratory analysis Bertopic may be used, which allows for the
   206	visualization and exploration of document landscapes.
   207	2.5. Reporting
   208	To support the reporting of studies, we suggest providing GPT
   209	with tabular results and result visualizations and request to highlight
   210	exciting patterns and observations in the data (e.g., with a specific focus
   211	on spotting research gaps).
   212	3. Reflections
   213	Study validity: Former studies have shown that GPT achieved similar
   214	or even better performance than humans. Publication bias and limited
   215	studies currently restrict our understanding of LLMs’ reliability in litera-
   216	ture reviews. The rapid evolution of LLMs, exemplified by Claude.ai and
   217	the upcoming GPT-o1, may quickly render current evaluations obsolete.
   218	This underscores the need for a conceptual framework to guide future
   219	research and applications of LLMs in software engineering (SE). Many
   220	existing studies are from outside SE, highlighting the importance of
   221	developing SE-specific solutions. We aim to inspire further research on
   222	LLMs’ performance in SE literature studies, acknowledging that findings
   223	from current models may not apply to future iterations.
   224	Complementary Tools: Tracing tools such asLangSmith allow for
   225	tracing (debugging, testing) individual steps in LLM-based systems,
   226	which is of use when engineering a systematic mapping tool. WebVoy-
   227	ager [10] can search the entire internet using a visual approach. As
   228	WebVoyager is not limited to research references, it may be of interest
   229	for grey literature reviews.
   230	We suggest two research directions:
   231	•Improving individual steps and evaluating different strategies
   232	(e.g., prompting) to optimize them.
   233	•Build a prototype representing the overall mapping process to
   234	gather further ideas for supporting mapping studies end-to-end.
